   "pages": [
    {
     "page": 1,
     "text": "ROBERTS-HORNE MEDICAL GROUP - DISCHARGE SUMMARY\n\nPatient: John Mccullough DOB: 07/11/1969 Sex: M MRN: 408297\n\nAdmit: 01/15/2002\nDischarge: 01/20/2026\n\nPrincipal diagnosis: Lung transplant recipient (24.3 years ago; organ other)\nSecondary: None\n\nDischarge diagnoses:\n1. Lung transplant recipient\n2. Back strain\n3. Upper respiratory infection\n4. Ankle sprain\n5. Rash\n\nHospital course:\nMr. Mccullough, a 56-year-old male, was admitted on 01/15/2026 primarily for follow-up evaluation as a lung transplant recipient. His transplant was performed 24.3 years ago. Mr. Mccullough has been doing well since his transplant with his condition currently controlled. \n\nDuring his hospital stay, Mr. Mccullough also presented with a back strain, which was managed conservatively with rest, physical therapy, and over-the-counter pain medications. He was also treated for an upper respiratory infection with a course of antibiotics, which resolved without complications. Additionally, he had a previous ankle sprain and a rash, both of which were noted but required no specific intervention during this admission.\n\nThroughout his hospital stay, Mr. Mccullough's vital signs remained stable. On his last recorded visit on 04/02/2026, his blood pressure was 112/77 mmHg, and his weight was 169 pounds. Previous visits showed consistent vital signs and weights, with blood pressures ranging from 102/76 mmHg to 118/83 mmHg and weights between 167 and 177 pounds.\n\nProcedures:\nNo specific procedures were performed during this admission. Management was primarily medical with supportive care for his symptoms.\n\nDischarge medications:\n1. Cyclosporine 100 mg daily\n2. Sirolimus 1 mg daily\n\nNote: It is important to highlight that Mr. Mccullough has been non-compliant with his medications. He has missed follow-up appointments on two occasions, which is concerning given his transplant status. Efforts will be made to improve his adherence to the medication regimen and regular follow-ups.\n\nSocial history:\nMr. Mccullough is a former cigarette smoker, having quit on 02/15/2017 after smoking one pack per day since 10/14/2011. He currently consumes approximately one alcoholic drink per week.\n\nFollow-up:\nMr. Mccullough is advised to follow up with his primary care physician, Dr. Maria Sanchez at Roberts-Horne Medical Group, for regular monitoring of his transplant status and overall health. He is encouraged to adhere strictly to his medication regimen and attend all scheduled appointments. Additionally, he should seek medical attention if he experiences any symptoms suggestive of transplant rejection or in"
    },
    {
     "page": 2,
     "text": "fection.\n\nIn summary, Mr. Mccullough was discharged home in stable condition after an uneventful hospital course. His primary diagnosis remains a lung transplant recipient, and secondary diagnoses include back strain, upper respiratory infection, ankle sprain, and rash. He is to continue on his current medication regimen, with a strong emphasis on medication compliance and regular follow-up appointments.\n"
    }
